  "name": "Question 13",
  "problem": "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent line to the curve $f(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\u20613x}{x}$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "We use the quotient rule with the numerator $u(x)=\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(3x)$ and the denominator $v(x)=x.$\n\nDifferentiating $u(x)$ and $v(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{\u2032}(x)=-3\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(3x),\\,{v}^{\u2032}(x)=1.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $u.$\n\nNow, applying the quotient rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{\u2032}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{\u2032}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-3\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(3x))(x)-(\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(3x))(1)}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3x\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(3x)-\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(3x)}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={f}^{\u2032}\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{-3(\\frac{\\pi }{2})sin\\,(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})-cos\\,(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})}{{(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3(\\frac{\\pi }{2})(-1)-0}{\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
